damaged_code:
```rust
struct SuffixFinder {
    prefix_index: Option<usize>,
    suffix_index: Option<usize>,
}

impl SuffixFinder {
    pub fn find_common_prefix(&mut self, expected: &str, actual: &str) {
        self.prefix_index = Some(calculate_prefix(expected, actual));
    }
    pub fn find_common_suffix(&mut self, expected: &str, actual: &str) {
        // Implicitly assumes find_common_prefix was called and updated prefix_index
        let idx = self.prefix_index.expect("Prefix must be calculated first!");
        self.suffix_index = Some(calculate_suffix(expected, actual, idx));
    }
}
```

counterexample:
Passing the output of the first calculation explicitly as an input to the second calculation:
```rust
impl SuffixFinder {
    pub fn find_common_prefix(&self, expected: &str, actual: &str) -> usize {
        calculate_prefix(expected, actual)
    }
    pub fn find_common_suffix(&self, expected: &str, actual: &str, prefix_idx: usize) -> usize {
        calculate_suffix(expected, actual, prefix_idx)
    }
}
```

fix:
Thread the intermediate state explicitly through the function signatures, or model the states using the type system (Type-Driven Development) so that invalid sequences do not compile.
```rust
struct PrefixResult {
    prefix_index: usize,
}

impl SuffixFinder {
    pub fn find_common_prefix(&self, expected: &str, actual: &str) -> PrefixResult {
        PrefixResult { prefix_index: calculate_prefix(expected, actual) }
    }
    pub fn find_common_suffix(&self, expected: &str, actual: &str, prefix: &PrefixResult) -> usize {
        calculate_suffix(expected, actual, prefix.prefix_index)
    }
}
```
